course: dictionary
lesson: 13043
title: Farce: funny chaos or a total mess
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A farce is a funny play or film full of silly situations. Things keep going wrong, people hide in cupboards, and mix-ups follow one after another. «The door opened, and the cat ran off with the birthday cake.» That kind of silliness is a farce.
explain: Grown-ups also use farce in everyday talk, in a different way. When something is so badly organised or unfair that it is ridiculous, people call it a farce. «The sports day was a farce: the races started late and the rain soaked everyone.» Here it does not mean funny ha-ha. It means a mess.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. In a play, words like chase, hide and mix-up show a farce. When people complain, words like badly organised, unfair and ridiculous show the second meaning. To check, swap in the word silly or ridiculous: if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: A farce is a type of comedy with fast action and unlikely events.
fact: Farces often have mix-ups, chases and people hiding in cupboards.
fact: «A farce» can also mean a situation that is so badly organised or unfair that it is ridiculous.
fact: The phrases «a complete farce» and «turn into a farce» mean that something has gone very badly.
fact: Farce is a noun: you can say «a farce» or «farces» when you talk about more than one.
exercise: «The play was full of chases, mix-ups and people hiding in cupboards.» What type of play is this? | *A farce | A sad story about a lost dog | A quiet poem about the moon
exercise: Dad says, «The school trip was a complete farce.» What does Dad mean? | *The trip was badly organised and ridiculous | The trip was the best day of the whole year | The trip was held on the moon
exercise: Find and copy the word that names a funny play full of silly mix-ups: «The class put on a farce about a lost hat.» | lost | class | *farce | hat
test: «The match became a farce when three players fell over at once.» What does farce mean here? | *A ridiculous, badly run mess | A famous football trophy | A kind of slow dance | A quiet, peaceful game
test: Which situation would best be called a farce? | A calm walk in the park on a sunny morning | A lesson where the teacher explains the plan clearly | *A play where a man hides in a cupboard while his dog steals his hat | A picnic with a tasty lunch and no rain
exam: Which sentence uses farce to mean a funny play? | *«The farce made the audience roar with laughter at the cupboard chase.» | «The farce of the vote was unfair, so the result was cancelled.» | Farce means a quiet, peaceful walk in the country.
